 The probability of getting heads in exactly $k$ out of 10 flips is given by the binomial distribution: $P(X = k) = \binom{10}{k} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{10}$. To find the probability of getting at least 6 heads, we need to sum the probabilities of getting exactly 6, 7, 8, 9, and 10 heads. \[
P(X \geq 6) = \sum_{k=6}^{10} \binom{10}{k} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{10}
\] After calculating, we get \[
P(X \geq 6) = \binom{10}{6} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{10} + \binom{10}{7} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{10} + \binom{10}{8} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{10} + \binom{10}{9} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{10} + \binom{10}{10} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{10} = \frac{193}{512}
\] So, the probability that we get heads in at least 6 of the 10 flips is \(\boxed{\frac{193}{512}}\). </think> <answer>193/512</answer>

